      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-09-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER,</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER,</p>
        <p>MT DIJA NIILL3I.</p>
        <p>The velvel chapeau is the proper thing for the autumn. It may be trimmed with any materiaL, net excepting cloth if the cloth be arranged in very smart way, but the velve..covered brim and crown ere expected. A lovely example of this mode in a wide-brimmed chapeau of turquoise blue velvet draped.with white gauze, and ornamented with aide bunch of white winge. l'ale yellow plume. are lovely on a hat of golden brown velvet with the brim turned up with a cluster of orange velvet flower.. The elm, to a walking hot of felt to be were with a tailor-made costume mom, to be an oddly-shaped rtunitte of some bright colour, orange or mararin blue being preferred, and this may be placed under the brim or holding a bunch of cock', feather', on the side. Naturally, velvet toques are aa popular aa velvet bate, bat hardly each • novelty.</p>
        <p>A quaint quaker bonnet imitable for • young matron, in trimmed in the softest and nweetest Made ot Arab velvet, with a little fluted brim of silk gauze. The crown in of the crimped gauze, with a top knot of velvet, and in the front a illogic loop of velvet mipporta white phone. The bodioe in dsy or evening disown in often quite different to the skirt in material or trimminge 1 but thee *leaven for the mmt part ahould be all the *in trimmingn—in groups of tuck, or braiding, in aloes rows of tiny tooks, in flounce., by. Had woollen fabrim will be trimmed with black aatrakan, for tailor virus Hunter green will be atilt • favourite shade ior oubiloor ilremen, though WWI en than lad year. Green and blue plaids will be men, but thin much-worn combination in non.. what out of date now sod red and black will take it. place. Dark heliotrope clothe are to be trimmed with black or grey furs</p>
        <p>Contrast. in eolone are very faehionalile. Ruppoe- in4 silk be used for a gown, the front might he of ohtehn net in smell il it pleats, fastened down with tiny ruched; of the same, and forming a small V•shayied yoke at the back In the IMITIO style. The silk comes from the shouldere on bible, and is drawn in a deep band of the silk at the waiet. Bodin. for brelal toilettes are mom effective in this fashion, with front and yoke of white chiffon, Um sleevea and sides, se also the deep hand, of white satin. A very handsome bridal robe mould in. made with • full bodies and long rotllol eleavee made entirely of chiffon, with a short belerti of Ivory satin, nearly covered with pearl and silver embroidery, and with little tabbed epaulette. to warrespond falling over the sleeve,</p>
        <p>All the smart evening gowns aro trained. A plain trained skirt of black gletr will he found an immense addition to • wardrobe. Some black grenadine or net bought at the eaten, trimmed two-thirds of the way up the skirt length witA graduated ends of black satin ribbon, and thee Sent to be sun-ray pleated, will make a charming ekirt to wear over this black glaoli of underskirt,</p>
        <p>A very besoming owtnine it made of reeeda anew with facings on the bodice of ivory-erhit• weird velour., braided finely in block and gold. The bodice fastens erith inlaid pearl button. ever • little 'int of tucked batiste, erhiolt has • etock collar and tie. Roiled the waiet ie a jewelled belt. .</p>
