        <p>etmnteri.ise that iian he erected i•tsinst the grolrtla of • huge military dospotiato which may ultimately threaten sofo.. very vitsl British inter. esteleith Eitrupo end Asia. That is • fact which wits re:linnet in former days by llsritsts• end other British statesmen, but what was ginned by their firm methods is now being ilropped from the nerveless Immix of Lori Ss user ue.</p>
        <p>It le ',crimpu orey toil lira/ that the transactions with France and the United State should excite more indignation in Tory circles than the aban- donment of Greene. France ia peraistently hostik• to the British occupation of Egypt, god tho Tories have always found none consolation in reminding the pimple acrms the channel of the feet that their oocupation of Tunis is just on • par with the °co:pa:Goo of Egypt. Yet, they ewoke up • few niorningx ago to llot that leirt SALSAS( IT has humbly ocktiowlodge.l all French claims Orotl TIROS without getting any quM tiro pu in regard to Egypt. In othor words our Fonatios Sara*. Tune has made • bargaill with Fratwo Which num. sista of giving everything out getting praeliadly nothing in return. Is it surprising therefore that the jingoe of the Primrose Leagon an almost be. aides themaelves with clis:nay. The American Mont is much inon, eeriou• from some pointeot view. Until recently Lord Ssusarev ha re. fused to re-open the Gehring lies sealing oitieetiam until it became dile next year according to the l'aris arbitration. In May laid, however, he re. CfliVed from Secretary SIIIMMAN probably the most Marina diplomatic docionent that hail ever been sent front ono country to siother, and almost atraightwaly Lnr,l Sat.:moor yield, to the American demand to at 01:05 re-open the ques- tion. This is • bad and dongerotis preaudent, mpecially as it follows upon the Venesuolus question.iii connection with which his hands were also forced by the American Government. These demand. from Uocle data mein to follow oath other with awkward persisteury, and if they an, to be eupportod by despatches liko that penned by Secretary finann•ii than, la just • danger that the relations iietween the two conntrim may suddenly reach the breaking point. A weak dle. poeition to yield at the first push is just the thing to encourage an err:wan:a that may soon liewune intolerable. Lord SALIM:kV will real;y have to show the foreign aboteernen who are sqosszing bus on every aide that hot. something more than is lath painted to look like iron."</p>
        <p>J•iits Werrff, secretary of the United Kingdom Athena*, hes Legislan en. publeched morn° information with regard to Sweden, Norway, not l'ariada which should be of Mtereet to toniperance people, isod indeed to all studied or cf tht !Voir. question Ile mentions that at the beginning of the pricieet century Sweden and Norway wore the meet drunker...tries in the wirld, nut that they are now amongst the ariber.t and heelthiest. This it, be conterels,• nark of stringent knot Ile shows by etatintics that when the ims- sumption nil apirita wax high the ikath-rato warn also high and that tho 0110 declined with the other. The new temperance luginlttion onion into foroe in Sweden in 1855, and Irma that time for. ward there has bowl • gradual domino in the death-rate front 217 per thousand of the poplar- tion to 17'5 in 1891. It may, of course, lei said that this improvoinent I. dim to better sari/. ration, but in Denmark, a kindred country, whore there has been little or nu diminution of the drinking habit.. of the couple the doathoate has in recant yearn ioily declined by It per loot en wimpwrvii with the 1-2 in 81s-cilia. Another Imi:wetly's fact in ifuntie! by Mr. W YTS. Before the temperance legislate. came into loroe in liwederi no fewer than thirty-six per rant id the conscripts drawn for military s •rvico bed to be rejected as physically unfit, bid by the Irmo the law had been in operation thirty yeara the pro- portion had beer, minced to twenty per cent' nein, as to Caned.. Thin colony hes the lightest drink-rate in Christendom and its death-rate it witually as low as 11-01 per 1,000. Quebec is tare province rn Cumule in which the temperance Mo•II- malt has made the least progrem and its rate:ie 1,1101, or 4.90 per I,ouo more then that of Canada as a whole.</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles did not gain anything by Ms Mo- tion let the rejection of the firabeams when* at the net meeting of the Warrington Board of (War- dle... The scheme woe adopted a month pre•louely by. Mitinty of one. it that niiii-ting it was imp. ported by • mai pelf y of two. That not bet for the Board of fluardians. Sr. Knowles to reelly •ti aliAtolng controvertible list tli. ono and ooly reason for ben&amp; tag forward his MOII,II was thst the members of the Board had acted like • lit of schoobboya and echoolgirla" in changing their minds with regard to the Bisbee°s scheme since last year ; :herder. be wanted them to again change their mind fro. month. It Moot surprising that lame arguniet is of this kiad had as effect tbe eery oppo•ite froa what was intended. Theo Mr. Knowles on Me meaning of words wee better still. In the runnel of his speech he referred to the inmates of the Workhome se tbe common heft ." sod in reply to, Mt. he maintained that tiNIOII was no insult fa this became. bard now; 't flock." Now, Mr. Knowlee is uodoubtedly wrong there. The two wurde are nut, by say *same always synonymous, and we do not advise gr. Knowle• lii talk to has rural comitituente Meat tt ft icks of cattle, or about their lochs "of swine, or le may lose cote.. Ile may. of owner, with perfect propnitty, speak about their “itucke •of gorse. I. the he,.. COMOIOII herd " imulting whim applied to it, Inmate. of • it ~elsh- oum ? li,. certainly nct ao-rnpiimenuarp fink.. sum defines the word herd to raean.wben applied be human beings. 't • crowd ~f iow people, a cabbie,' and Dryden coetestiptuou•ly refers to • cort•ln rime of persona gi</p>
