        <p>MD cl1:41 Inuemo6 ..... • • tal ill the town. Ohio map accordingly dene,and during the next foe .lass hit condition imposed, to much m that strong h were entertained of his recovery. On Nunday,tinirever.the patient underwent • change for the worm, and expired early on Monday morning, elhertly after the laat nu...of the Choreh had teen administered. The Rev. Joaeph F., Turner, 011 8., was horn in 1853, and ma. at the time of hie death forty. four years of age. Ho ems • native of Poston, and punned his encleinaatlcal studies at Ampleforth College. Y.skshire lie received tile Benedictine habit In 1872, and was ordained priest in 1880 at It. Benedict*. MoLaderl, Fort Adiguetaa, on the banks of Loch Net., Intl,. founding of which home he tonk • leading part. Father T saintly after hit ordlnetim returned to A mpleforth to Mins charge of the choir. He remained here for some tinir , until Me tranalerenm to Ni. Dal if ehembee. A few year. later saw him m- iaowed to Workington, Cumberland. He came to St. Mary's, Warrington, in 1891. awl remained here two years. Ills kindly and sunny dimly/100cm endeared him to ell. He ma. a znost earnest and energetic worker, and his indelatigahle labours on to half of the League of the (7ries will king be remembered with gra.r. I ulness. It araa in him that the meinheia '.me the re- rematino el the premise.. leer a time he had charge of the schools, and was great favourite with 'ha children ia whom weefare he took a deep interest. lie C•rnpowd Iwo nperet,s. " Z4lebrw. if.. King of the Cannibal islands," and ° Wanted, the nom of • happy roan," which were performed by the &amp;ahead children tithe Royal Court 'Theatre, the gr-ain 1891 and the eecond in Ike following year, and they were pronounced to be of very blab merit. The performances at all event. were eminently successful, and Charles Melville, the well-known actor, who waa prment at the performance el the second operetta. w•a enthusiastic in his admiratinn ni thy Pi,e mid the