        <p>GOOO TRY pcm.ty.</p>
        <p>The tumid weekly mission of the Hope of Barlett- town Lodge wan held in the Crenperativo Ball on Monday evening. Bro. 'l'. Cutler, CT., prodded over a largo attendance of member, and mritors. Two candidates were admitted to memberehip and several propoaed. Arrangements worn made for • visit to the Wesley Lodge, Liverpool, on Friday, September '24,b, to give an entertainment, Brox. Taylor and W. Willis to be renioniii tile for the Pro- gramme. It was decided to have • "social gather- tog" at the next meet'ng of the Lielge,refreshmente to be supplied. Bro. Anders and hie wife were pre- sent as visitore from Sr. !felons, and they addreinied the Lodge, stating that they had come to reside at Earleatown. The programme for the evening was .10140a1 Temperance Tithe Water, what it is and what it does; compiled op Pro. 3. B. (filings, ever' member taking part. An interettiug evening closed in the .11111 i way. -</p>
        <p>DZ.; OF M n..Trom As Prom</p>
        <p>The announcement at the beginning of this week of the death of Mr. Thomaa Stone, fifth von of rho late Mr. Thomas Stone of Newton Park. was re- ceived with considerable surprise in Karlestown and Newton whore the decenseil Kennon:MO was •ery well-known.Mr.F.tone was at one time quite a familiar figure in this neirlitiourhood, where his activity in conneCtion with the Conservative cause brought him very much into promineneo. Nine sears ago he left the family roof for Clued a where he has since permuted the milling of a farmer on • ! largo ovate. Lamt winter he had an attack of pni.u• monis from which, however, he somewhat re- covered. For some little time latterly he he/ been In indifferent health, and Mr. Robert Stone, Iris brother, went out to tanada nearly three week, ago to see him. The family hail little idea that his ill- nine Was so serious or that the earl war so near. Mr. Hobert Stone srrive.l at Calgary where Mr. Thomas Stone raeided, en Thursday lot, and found his brother very weak, but perfectly eonreloiti, and remained with him np to the lash On Wednesday afternoon a memori•l mei-vire wan held at Winevick Church conducted by the Ite,. Canon l'ourhyn. ashioted by the Rev. If Monk. There wan a large number of friends and sympethisers present and the service wan of an improssi V.l character. Amongst those in the Church were, Mr John -tone, Mr. Walter Stone, Mr. George Stone (brothers), Nlrs. Hardman (sire'). Mei... John Stone, Mims J. C. Stone, (niece). Mr. T. .ad Mr. h. W. Stone (nephews), Mr. T. J. ((Menlo, Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Dr. and Mrs. llandyside, Dr. Street, Mr. Hartley Bibby Li•er• pool), Mr. W. S. Biotin (Liverpool), Mr. li. Bieber- ten (Liverpool), Mr. R. B. Mem hews, (Manchester), Mr. G. Clarke, Mr. James Fairclough, Mr. L. Fair- eloogh (Barrington), I )sptat n Lowe anil eon ( Liver- pool), her, J. W. and If. Wat kins, Mr. J. L. Wool, Mr, F.. 1, Wood, Rev W. and Mrs. Williams, Messrs E. and I'. Fairclough, Mr. W. Mawdeley, Mr. W. Thompson, Mr. J. Bell (Wmnfoenh Mr. Collier, Mr. J. Boardman, Mr. Joseph Boarlmen, Mr, Longton, Mr. 11. J. Longton. Mr. Hamlett t Farm Bailiff, and a number of the lam servant.. The .I,easeil gentleman wam a t liorough athlete tail he always took the greatest Interest in any atblotic gathering in Ihe (Works, and as • averts- man ho was e.onsider,el a creek hh et. Knowing hi( Hoe athletic build, tire new, of his death came am • shook upon the many people who were acquainted with hica.</p>
