        <p>The eery of • remerkable felt ceinee from California, A great illeCkhg of triount•la was regent', t,ral off by 10,0110Iha of powder, lifted several feet ',eight up awl than pu•hed north,. friryard 40 or 50 fon, then falling with an awful rote 125 foot, to remain hereafter for all time as the bulwark of the great dein firing built to jot. Pound water For the city of San Francis,. On the eur. face and in plaew through the mountain side WPM placed hig depoeite of giant powder fir the purpnw of shattering the twain and lifting it up. According to plane, the pond.. when it exploded would hurl the NOM Straight forwent, making a bridge of granite &amp;ern., the gorge and blrriki, the stream. The plans were carried out with the grnateet care ant with a IPICCP44III nand, ' When the dust cleared away it an found that the Nast had dislnolged • lava, of rock 400 feet up and down amain and an ..... of 60 feet in height, completelr , bridging the canyon. rhn eagineers eatirnated that the amoult dislodged weighed about 150,0)) tn..</p>
        <p>Th,re Was no leek 411 supporters of the Warring. ton Club at Wigan on Saturday in fact they forme] no inconsderehle proportion of the roreta- • tore who lined the Wigen enclosure. Tine syne a eource of encouragement to the Wigan committee, who are at prevent bring hard pocked by the Wig. I County Association Club, which by means of • • superb ground, and a gist-clans team is making a t bold bid for the apectatnrs' support. There were two special excursion trains from Warrington, one ' run by the London and North•Wondern Railway i• Company, and the other by the Cheshire Lines Com- mittee. The Warrington team travelled by the Cheehtre Lined and were accompanied by • grind • number of spectator, It war the first match of the twason on the Wigan ground, which seenied in • better condition than usual. The weather was I propitious, and there were pleasnrable anticipa- t Ho. of a fart and even game In the roitels of the opectaters. Despite the had start ma 1 o by Wig., who up to last Saturday bed boon stony defeated in both their league engagements this gess., there watt still a strong feeling amongst the followers of the cherry and st tire that I'Verrington would not be able to lower the colours of the Wigan., when on their native heath. The Warrington spectators, on the other hand, felt trial their team had only to play euch another g•tne an was witneetted on the Wilderep.l enclosure again. Rochdale the previous Sat urdey, to owner.. victory. Thee wee very little alteration In the Warrington team, the most di...penning feature being the sheen. of Barber, which mooed some misgiving on the part of the opectators. Hie place was by If igginbottom. The Wigan team is far from be- ing organised at present, end almat up to the last moment on Saturday, there was great uncertainty Otto who would be included in the team. When the players did appear it We. noticed that Hugh.. and Burton who were expected to appear were amongst the absent ones. TWO new faces were seen in Holm., the North countryman, and Rhoden,whn at nee time was agrociatod with the late Aepull club The teams faced eactn other ay follow. Wigao : Winstanley, full back Rhoden, Jago, Holmes, itnbinenn, three-quarter hacks; Walkden and Dixon half-back.; Rigby, Collier, Webster Blackburn, Walsh, CatteralL Benson, Talon, for, wards. Warringten : Bascow, full back Higginbotteni, Cross, Idlers.od and fleeces three-quarter backs ; Bate and nought., half-backs ; Taylor, Dakin, Naylor, Schultz,, Feirhurod, Barrett, Swift. and !Ingham, forwards. Mr. MeCischenn was for the third time in ...shin referee in the Warringron match.</p>
        <p>I..:.;mnsencing with the sun behind them, the Warrington men warted none too promieingly, but •fter • brief intotaion of Warrington territory, mid. Acid wa, again reached, where give and take play ennueil a short time. There was oo ennapicnoun play in the early atagen of the rll•tett, With the ex- ception, perhaps, Ott dirtier piece of play by Bale, who stopped. daring rush of the hon. forwards In the ncrimmegen the Warrington team hold the advantage, and the half-backs got posbiesion of the ball tire tintem ma of every ow. They did not. how- ever, matte the beat ace of their opportunities, and Dixon,wlin played. great game for the home team, neltitteul many of the efforts of the Warrington men. Rhoden nod Holms, both tried to break l through the Warrington IterftoCf). bust Wit hoot avail, the waling of the visiting team being smart and safe. Al tepee, I•herwood, Crow, and Bate were chiefly instrumental In transferring the yenuse of play to Wigan quarters, and nn ressral mg... they were in near proximity to the home goal line. The prem.,. wan continued for some I ime, but try as the Wartington men did, they could not record anything tangible. Relief Clllll4 to the Wigan players ant op • cwt.r, alike when the home forivard• worked the hall out of I danger. and by esay stager, play was trannferretl beyond the hell-way line. Almost immediately after, iIIICCOSX attended the effort. of the home team. A scrimmage wan lormrtl ear the Warring- ton 25, and thn bail having been throws. in, it rebounded into Dixon's. hand,. Houghton, think- ing Ito hall hail nut been in the acrimmage, allowed Dix in to pa. him. The result was dia.troun to Warrington, for a timely pass to Holmes enabled that player to anore. Higginbottont failing to push him into touch when near the line. Winstanley , endeavoured 'n Improve the score, but did not 'we- e...Land the reteroe'm whistle noon afterwachi an. nuanced that balfetime hfof arrived.</p>
        <p>Fr rn the mama, the Warrington non buckled to their work in line fashioti,and the Wigan players were kept homy defending their lutes. • free kink being allowed to Warrington, Taylor made a aocet attempt too senee, the hall just falling •bort. the kick oat, Warrington again ilesumed the agres• ales, hut failed to score at the critical 0.01060 t. their effort, ending on point lens touch-olewne. Pfor at least half-an-hour in the second portion of the (ZlOO.. the Wigan moon were pinned in their own Iguerters. bui my mookiing and a little luck they ...led in preventing any 00010 by the visitors. The first emit into Warrington quarters remolt,ed in on Uninrttlallte net dent. Cross won in the act et catching tumor ball from • high kick by one of the on,- posing hacks when a dashing charge wee made tipon him by several of the Wigan forwsrols, reeult- log in such an injury that lie had to Inane the field, and may be unabie to play for some time to Come, iiooooloo filled thin vacancy, and Naylor woe brought out of the futwarda to accompany fate behind the pack. A hrilli- •nt run by Houghton, which replayed immewhat his errors in the earlier part of th • game transferred play to the Wigan 25. Dixon secured after Borne lanoi, play, and was within an ace of mooring when hew.. tackled by Naylor. The lent stages of the game were contested near the Wigan hne,hat nothing further woe scored, and the nound of the whistle came as a welcome riolool, tlie game being far from what may be termed interesting. Wigan were the fortunate winners of the match by a try to nil.</p>
